<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Optimization with Lagrange Multipliers: Finding Extrema with a Constraint</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Find the maximum and the minimum values of \(f(x,y)={@a@}x^2+{@c@}y^2+{@e@}\) subject to the constraint \(g(x,y)={@b@}x^2+{@c@}y^2={@d@}\) using the <a href="https://tutorial.math.lamar.edu/Classes/CalcIII/LagrangeMultipliers.aspx">Method of Lagrange Multipliers</a>. By completing the following solution</p>
<p>First, we observe that the constraint region is bounded. It is also closed, therefore it is [[input:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]] Now, by the Extreme Value Theorem, we can conclude that the function [[input:ans2]][[feedback:prt2]]  in the constraint region [[validation:ans1]] [[validation:ans2]]</p>
<p>The next step is to write down the system of Lagrange Multiplier equations:</p>
<p>[[input:ans3]][[feedback:prt3]] \((f_x=\lambda g_x)...\) equation \(1\) [[validation:ans3]]</p>
<p>[[input:ans4]][[feedback:prt4]] \((f_y=\lambda g_y)...\) equation \(2\) [[validation:ans4]]</p>
<p>[[input:ans5]][[feedback:prt5]] (Constraint equation) \(…\) equation \(3\) [[validation:ans5]]</p>
<p>From the second equation</p>
<p>\(y=\) [[input:ans6]][[feedback:prt6]] (<b>case </b>\(1\)) or \(\lambda=\)[[input:ans7]][[feedback:prt7]] (<b>case </b>\(2\)) [[validation:ans6]] [[validation:ans7]]</p>
<p><b>Case </b>\(1:\)  Plugging in the value of \(y\) into the constraint equation</p>
<p>\(x=\pm\) [[input:ans8]][[validation:ans8]][[feedback:prt8]]</p>
<p>Hence two potential absolute extrema points are
</p>
<p>[[input:ans9]][[feedback:prt9]] and [[input:ans10]][[validation:ans9]][[validation:ans10]]</p>
<p><b>Case </b>\(2:\) Plugging in the value of \(\lambda\) into equation \(1\)</p>
<p>\(x=\) [[input:ans11]][[validation:ans11]][[feedback:prt11]]</p>
<p>Plugging in the value of \(x\) into the constraint equation</p>
<p>\(y=\pm\) [[input:ans12]][[validation:ans12]][[feedback:prt12]]</p>
<p>This part gives us two more points that are potential absolute extrema,</p>
<p>[[input:ans13]][[feedback:prt13]] and [[input:ans14]][[validation:ans13]][[validation:ans14]]</p>
<p>To find the absolute extrema we evaluate the function at each of these points</p>
<p>The absolute maximum is then [[input:ans15]][[validation:ans15]][[feedback:prt15]]</p>
<p>The absolute minimum is [[input:ans16]][[validation:ans16]][[feedback:prt16]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>Here the constraint is a sum of two terms that are squared, hence the largest possible range of \(x\) is \(-{@lar_xr@}\le {@lar_xr@}\) (the largest values occur if \(y=0.\)</p>
<p>Likewise,the largest possible range of \(y\) is \(-\displaystyle {@lar_yr@}\le \displaystyle{@lar_yr@}\)  (with the largest values occurring if \(x=0\)).</p>
<p>Therefore, the constraint region is bounded. It is also closed, therefore it is <b>compact</b>. Now, by the Extreme Value Theorem, we can conclude that the function <b>has absolute extrema</b> in the constraint region</p>
<p>The next step is to write down the system of Lagrange Multiplier equations:</p>
<p>\({@f_x@}\) \((f_x=\displaystyle \lambda g_x)...\) equation \(1\)</p>
<p>\({@f_y@}\) \((f_y=\displaystyle \lambda g_y)...\) equation \(2\) </p>
<p>\({@g_xy@}\) (Constraint equation) \(…\) equation \(3\) </p>
<p>From the second equation</p>
<p>\({@d3@}\displaystyle \lambda-{@d4@}={@d4@}(\displaystyle \lambda-1)\)</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \rightarrow y=0\) (<b>case </b>\(1\)) or \(\lambda=1\)(<b>case </b>\(2\))</p>
<p><b>Case </b>\(1:\)  when \(y=0\) </p>
<p>\({@b@}x^2={@d@} \rightarrow x=\displaystyle \pm {@ta8@}\)</p>
<p>Hence two potential absolute extrema points are
</p>
<p>\((-{@lar_xr@},0)\) and \(({@lar_xr@},0)\)</p>
<p><b>Case </b>\(2:\) when \(\displaystyle \lambda =1\)</p>
<p>\({@d1@}={@d2@}\displaystyle  \rightarrow x={@ta11@}\)</p>
<p>Plugging in \(x=0\) into the constraint equation</p>
<p>\({@c@}y^2=\displaystyle {@d@} \rightarrow 
y=\displaystyle \pm {@ta12@}\)</p>
<p>This part gives us two more points that are potential absolute extrema,</p>
<p>\((0,-\displaystyle {@ta12@})\) and \((0,\displaystyle {@ta12@})\)</p>
<p>To find the absolute extrema we evaluate the function at each of these points</p>
<p>\(f(-{@lar_xr@},0)=\displaystyle {@val_x1y@}\)  \(f({@lar_xr@},0)={@val_x2y@}\)  \(f(0,-{@ta12@})={@val_y1x@}\)  \(f(0,{@ta12@})=\displaystyle {@val_y2x@}\)</p>
<p>The absolute maximum is then \({@ta15@}\)</p>
<p>The absolute minimum is \({@ta16@}\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
